00:04:32.315 --> 00:04:33.940
MARK MANDEL: Let's
talk about the chip.

00:04:33.940 --> 00:04:36.309
What exactly does the chip do?

00:04:36.309 --> 00:04:37.850
NEAL MUELLER: Let's
take a step back.

00:04:37.850 --> 00:04:41.346
Before we start getting into
the nerdery of this security

00:04:41.346 --> 00:04:41.970
microprocessor.

00:04:41.970 --> 00:04:42.490
FRANCESC CAMPOY: We're
just very excited.

00:04:42.490 --> 00:04:44.290
MARK MANDEL: I'm just
really excited by the nerdy.

00:04:44.290 --> 00:04:45.745
NEAL MUELLER: Let's
tell an analogy.

00:04:45.745 --> 00:04:47.661
I bet there's more people
in the audience that

00:04:47.661 --> 00:04:51.910
have seen the movie "Inception"
than understand what a security

00:04:51.910 --> 00:04:53.662
microprocessor is,
which is what Titan is.

00:04:53.662 --> 00:04:55.370
So if you've seen the
movie "Inception"--

00:04:55.370 --> 00:04:57.220
MARK MANDEL: And if you have
not, you should go see it.

00:04:57.220 --> 00:04:58.310
Incredible movie about--

00:04:58.310 --> 00:04:59.050
CORNELIUS WILLIS:
You kind of have

00:04:59.050 --> 00:05:01.490
to see that in order to--
if you haven't seen it,

00:05:01.490 --> 00:05:03.670
you've got to stop
listening to the podcast.

00:05:03.670 --> 00:05:05.492
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
I'd say so, yeah.

00:05:05.492 --> 00:05:06.950
NEAL MUELLER: So
"Inception" is all

00:05:06.950 --> 00:05:10.661
about this thing in philosophy
called simulation theory.

00:05:10.661 --> 00:05:13.160
And it's the disconnection and
the concern about there being

00:05:13.160 --> 00:05:15.560
a disconnection between
your perception of reality

00:05:15.560 --> 00:05:18.410
and reality itself, that you
might actually be living inside

00:05:18.410 --> 00:05:21.020
of somebody else's dreams,
somebody else's Sim City,

00:05:21.020 --> 00:05:23.390
and it's not actually
your own little life.

00:05:23.390 --> 00:05:25.190
That's exactly the
concern that Google

00:05:25.190 --> 00:05:28.120
has with regard to our
hardware and our software.

00:05:28.120 --> 00:05:29.870
We want to make sure
that the relationship

00:05:29.870 --> 00:05:32.161
between our hardware and our
software comes up in a way

00:05:32.161 --> 00:05:33.450
that we consider trusted.

00:05:33.450 --> 00:05:35.450
And that's why we have a
security microprocessor

00:05:35.450 --> 00:05:37.430
that we called Titan
that authenticates

00:05:37.430 --> 00:05:38.600
this relationship.

00:05:38.600 --> 00:05:40.370
Every time we push
a new piece of BIOS,

00:05:40.370 --> 00:05:42.500
which is software
located on the hardware,

00:05:42.500 --> 00:05:44.404
Titan authenticates
that installation.

00:05:44.404 --> 00:05:46.820
And every single time that
hardware boots up some software

00:05:46.820 --> 00:05:49.220
that we've given it, it
authenticates that boot-up.

00:05:49.220 --> 00:05:51.680
And it allows us to have,
like Cornelius said,

00:05:51.680 --> 00:05:53.390
authenticated
service-to-service-level

00:05:53.390 --> 00:05:55.556
communication between our
hardware and our software,

00:05:55.556 --> 00:05:57.830
and also between our
software in our software.

00:05:57.830 --> 00:06:00.085
It's really similar
in theory to the stuff

00:06:00.085 --> 00:06:01.460
that we also have
in our security

00:06:01.460 --> 00:06:04.390
keys that every Googler
has on their computer.

00:06:04.390 --> 00:06:06.710
Insofar as we believe at
Google that everything

00:06:06.710 --> 00:06:08.210
should have an identity.

00:06:08.210 --> 00:06:10.970
Our hardware has identity,
our software has identity.

00:06:10.970 --> 00:06:16.430
We live in a constant state that
we consider always untrusted

00:06:16.430 --> 00:06:18.950
and we verify the identity of
everything all of the time.

00:06:18.950 --> 00:06:22.670
And that way, if anything in our
infrastructure is deemed rogue,

00:06:22.670 --> 00:06:25.310
we can very quickly de-identify
it and then carry on

00:06:25.310 --> 00:06:26.087
with business.

00:06:26.087 --> 00:06:27.920
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So I'm
wondering, does that

00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:31.790
mean that every single
one of those little chips

00:06:31.790 --> 00:06:34.580
has some secret
built-in in there,

00:06:34.580 --> 00:06:38.510
like [? YubiKey, ?]
something that at any point

00:06:38.510 --> 00:06:42.080
you can decide which one is
the one that is not a good one

00:06:42.080 --> 00:06:44.000
and you just stop
trusting the machine

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:46.024
or whatever piece of
hardware you have?

00:06:46.024 --> 00:06:48.440
NEAL MUELLER: Yes, so how do
you know that the type itself

00:06:48.440 --> 00:06:49.400
is the real type?

00:06:49.400 --> 00:06:51.320
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
because you have many of them.

00:06:51.320 --> 00:06:52.130
So where did you start--

00:06:52.130 --> 00:06:53.450
MARK MANDEL: Let's go deeper.

00:06:53.450 --> 00:06:56.000
FRANCESC CAMPOY: It's turtles,
all the way down, let's.

00:06:56.000 --> 00:06:58.460
NEAL MUELLER: We fuse
each individual Titan chip

00:06:58.460 --> 00:06:59.636
with a unique identifier.

00:06:59.636 --> 00:07:01.010
And this doesn't
mean the writing

00:07:01.010 --> 00:07:04.107
that was on the top, although
we did remove that when

00:07:04.107 --> 00:07:06.440
we gave it to Urs and let him
make an earring out of it.

00:07:06.440 --> 00:07:08.330
CORNELIUS WILLIS: It wasn't
good on the jewelery, anyway.

00:07:08.330 --> 00:07:09.494
NEAL MUELLER: No,
it's not out of style.

00:07:09.494 --> 00:07:09.710
It's gauche.

00:07:09.710 --> 00:07:11.168
CORNELIUS WILLIS:
We were competing

00:07:11.168 --> 00:07:13.070
with the diamond stud.

00:07:13.070 --> 00:07:14.970
So we had the
little numbers now.

00:07:14.970 --> 00:07:17.780
NEAL MUELLER: The fusing happens
inside the read-only memory

00:07:17.780 --> 00:07:19.080
of the chip itself.

00:07:19.080 --> 00:07:21.770
And it's done
sequentially so that we

00:07:21.770 --> 00:07:24.500
can tell that it's a Titan
chip that's in our inventory

00:07:24.500 --> 00:07:27.440
sequence and
theoretically is only

00:07:27.440 --> 00:07:29.750
being able to be
written once and can't

00:07:29.750 --> 00:07:32.450
be changed within the
chip during the chip's

00:07:32.450 --> 00:07:33.470
entire lifetime.

00:07:33.470 --> 00:07:35.444
So you know that that
is the real McCoy.

00:07:35.444 --> 00:07:36.860
MARK MANDEL: And
just to be clear,

00:07:36.860 --> 00:07:38.152
we make the chips, right?

00:07:38.152 --> 00:07:39.110
CORNELIUS WILLIS: Yeah.

00:07:39.110 --> 00:07:40.890
A lot of our stuff,
we control the design

00:07:40.890 --> 00:07:41.900
and the manufacturer.

00:07:41.900 --> 00:07:45.185
That's so we are able to ensure
to the greatest degree possible

00:07:45.185 --> 00:07:47.324
it has not been tampered with.

00:07:47.324 --> 00:07:49.490
NEAL MUELLER: This is one
of the big differentiators

00:07:49.490 --> 00:07:52.220
of our cloud versus
every other cloud

00:07:52.220 --> 00:07:54.590
that I've been able to
learn anything about.

00:07:54.590 --> 00:07:57.470
There are other folks that run
data centers, and other clouds

00:07:57.470 --> 00:07:59.480
as well, and they
can buy servers

00:07:59.480 --> 00:08:02.390
that have security
microprocessors on them.

00:08:02.390 --> 00:08:04.190
The majority of those
that I've talked to

00:08:04.190 --> 00:08:06.890
don't use those security
microprocessors.

00:08:06.890 --> 00:08:09.170
In fact, no cloud that
I've been able to talk to

00:08:09.170 --> 00:08:11.210
uses their security
microprocessors

00:08:11.210 --> 00:08:14.630
that are given to
them by other vendors.

00:08:14.630 --> 00:08:18.320
Not only do we use those,
but we created our own,

00:08:18.320 --> 00:08:22.190
because it's not our jam to
not understand fully the supply

00:08:22.190 --> 00:08:23.630
chain.

00:08:23.630 --> 00:08:26.300
So we purpose-build our
servers, and our chips,

00:08:26.300 --> 00:08:29.480
and our security
microprocessors, and our racks,

00:08:29.480 --> 00:08:31.730
and our network controllers,
as you saw in the picture

00:08:31.730 --> 00:08:33.169
when Urs was up on stage.

00:08:33.169 --> 00:08:35.150
We want to fully
understand the supply chain

00:08:35.150 --> 00:08:37.070
so that we can
guarantee provenance

00:08:37.070 --> 00:08:38.630
over the security of our cloud.

00:08:38.630 --> 00:08:40.296
CORNELIUS WILLIS:
It's not just identity

00:08:40.296 --> 00:08:41.750
that we're trying
to authenticate,

00:08:41.750 --> 00:08:43.639
but also heritage.

00:08:43.639 --> 00:08:44.930
Where did that thing come from?

00:08:44.930 --> 00:08:45.812
Provenance.

00:08:45.812 --> 00:08:48.270
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, it sounds
like it's the whole thing too,

00:08:48.270 --> 00:08:50.090
from BIOS all the way
through to software.

00:08:50.090 --> 00:08:52.402
Is it doing the thing we
think it's meant to be doing?

00:08:52.402 --> 00:08:53.110
And if it's not--

00:08:53.110 --> 00:08:54.485
CORNELIUS WILLIS:
And perpetually

00:08:54.485 --> 00:08:56.250
challenge that across
the entire system.

00:08:56.250 --> 00:08:58.310
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So to
