00:10:41.300 --> 00:10:45.080
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Going back to
the geekness area, I'm curious.

00:10:45.080 --> 00:10:48.290
So this is a very
useful thing to have.

00:10:48.290 --> 00:10:50.240
The fact that you're
able to authenticate

00:10:50.240 --> 00:10:52.970
a piece of hardware, it
is amazing for us, Google.

00:10:52.970 --> 00:10:56.030
But it might also be amazing
for other people, everyone

00:10:56.030 --> 00:10:56.802
in the world.

00:10:56.802 --> 00:10:58.760
Is this something that
is offered as a service?

00:10:58.760 --> 00:11:00.950
Or it's just like
a security layer

00:11:00.950 --> 00:11:02.327
that is internal to Google?

00:11:02.327 --> 00:11:04.160
NEAL MUELLER: Right now
we're the only cloud

00:11:04.160 --> 00:11:06.530
that we know of that's
currently using a security

00:11:06.530 --> 00:11:08.360
microprocessor.

00:11:08.360 --> 00:11:10.610
Not only are we the only
one using one, period,

00:11:10.610 --> 00:11:13.220
but we're the only one
that has purpose built one.

00:11:13.220 --> 00:11:17.720
If we were to make this chip
addressable for customers,

00:11:17.720 --> 00:11:20.960
configurable for customers,
we would be the first cloud

00:11:20.960 --> 00:11:21.770
to do so.

00:11:21.770 --> 00:11:23.100
We don't currently do that.

00:11:23.100 --> 00:11:24.020
MARK MANDEL: OK.

00:11:24.020 --> 00:11:24.830
That's fair enough.

00:11:24.830 --> 00:11:26.630
I think this is also
very interesting,

00:11:26.630 --> 00:11:28.850
the whole question of
the cloud, and this often

00:11:28.850 --> 00:11:32.269
comes up where it's like,
hey, is the cloud secure?

00:11:32.269 --> 00:11:34.310
And then we get that sort
of thing like, oh, god,

00:11:34.310 --> 00:11:35.900
we can't move
things to the cloud.

00:11:35.900 --> 00:11:37.040
NEAL MUELLER: This is one
of those great examples

00:11:37.040 --> 00:11:38.150
of, oh, my gosh--

00:11:38.150 --> 00:11:39.980
what did Eric
Schmidt say on stage?

00:11:39.980 --> 00:11:42.474
He was like, [INAUDIBLE]
the cloud now.

00:11:42.474 --> 00:11:44.640
CORNELIUS WILLIS: And don't
try to do this yourself,

00:11:44.640 --> 00:11:45.765
it's a waste of your money.

00:11:45.765 --> 00:11:48.348
MARK MANDEL: But it seems like
this is the sort of thing where

00:11:48.348 --> 00:11:51.080
you're like, is this the type of
security you would do yourself

00:11:51.080 --> 00:11:52.670
if you're building
stuff on your own,

00:11:52.670 --> 00:11:54.042
like data centers or networks?

00:11:54.042 --> 00:11:56.000
How much effort do you
want to put in yourself?

00:11:56.000 --> 00:11:58.270
It must have been a huge
investment to make this--

00:11:58.270 --> 00:11:59.936
CORNELIUS WILLIS: And
a huge investment,

00:11:59.936 --> 00:12:01.610
not only in money, but in time.

00:12:01.610 --> 00:12:02.914
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, research.

00:12:02.914 --> 00:12:04.580
CORNELIUS WILLIS:
Many, many, many years

00:12:04.580 --> 00:12:07.015
of building out the
layered architecture.

00:12:07.015 --> 00:12:10.034
And then it hasn't stopped.

00:12:10.034 --> 00:12:11.450
NEAL MUELLER: You
might be curious

00:12:11.450 --> 00:12:15.110
what goes into a security
microprocessor like Titan.

00:12:15.110 --> 00:12:16.820
So within Titan
are three things--

00:12:16.820 --> 00:12:19.820
a random number generator,
a crypto engine,

00:12:19.820 --> 00:12:21.830
and a monotonic counter.

00:12:21.830 --> 00:12:24.230
So of those three,
the one that I really

00:12:24.230 --> 00:12:26.990
didn't understand until I
googled it was monotonic

00:12:26.990 --> 00:12:27.800
counter.

00:12:27.800 --> 00:12:29.360
And now that I
learned what it means,

00:12:29.360 --> 00:12:31.070
I'm super excited to share.

00:12:31.070 --> 00:12:33.440
A monotonic counter
is essentially

00:12:33.440 --> 00:12:36.050
something that protects
against replay attacks,

00:12:36.050 --> 00:12:38.540
in addition to other
threat vectors.

00:12:38.540 --> 00:12:42.020
And it ensures that a count
goes forward and never backward.

00:12:42.020 --> 00:12:46.779
It's a tool within logs
to make tamper evident.

00:12:46.779 --> 00:12:49.070
Because if you were ever to
remove a log and say, oops,

00:12:49.070 --> 00:12:51.410
that never happened, that
person never came here,

00:12:51.410 --> 00:12:52.910
it would be obvious
because it would

00:12:52.910 --> 00:12:55.070
go from two to four to five.

00:12:55.070 --> 00:12:56.870
It'd be obvious that
you removed something.

00:12:56.870 --> 00:12:58.010
And if you tried
to insert something

00:12:58.010 --> 00:12:59.840
and say something happened
when it actually didn't, the

00:12:59.840 --> 00:13:01.820
monotonic counter
would be proof positive

00:13:01.820 --> 00:13:03.260
that it had not occurred.

00:13:03.260 --> 00:13:06.064
Those are the three things
inside a secure microprocessor.

00:13:06.064 --> 00:13:06.980
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:13:06.980 --> 00:13:10.550
So we said what goes
inside of Titan, talked

00:13:10.550 --> 00:13:13.100
about what it's used for.

00:13:13.100 --> 00:13:15.879
Where does it actually
go other than on ears?

00:13:15.879 --> 00:13:16.920
NEAL MUELLER: Yeah, sure.

00:13:16.920 --> 00:13:19.984
So the position
is very important

00:13:19.984 --> 00:13:21.150
for this particular product.

00:13:21.150 --> 00:13:23.730
It needs to sit
between ROM and RAM

00:13:23.730 --> 00:13:27.170
in order to provide a signature
validation of the first eight

00:13:27.170 --> 00:13:29.590
kilobits of installation
of a BIOS, for example,

00:13:29.590 --> 00:13:34.400
or the boot-up of any of
our pieces of hardware.

00:13:34.400 --> 00:13:37.670
And once it validates
the first eight kilobytes

00:13:37.670 --> 00:13:40.690
of installation, it lets you
install the rest of the BIOS

00:13:40.690 --> 00:13:42.305
and in every machine boot.

00:13:42.305 --> 00:13:43.221
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:13:43.221 --> 00:13:46.430
So one last question.

