aren't instructions,
super of obnoxious.

00:18:44.170 --> 00:18:47.680
So when we talk to customers,
every single one says, I just

00:18:47.680 --> 00:18:49.810
want you to tell me how
I'm supposed to do it.

00:18:49.810 --> 00:18:51.230
So we're trying to do that.

00:18:51.230 --> 00:18:52.270
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, and
it's so much better

00:18:52.270 --> 00:18:53.650
to learn from the
mistakes of others than--

00:18:53.650 --> 00:18:54.100
MILES WARD: That's right.

00:18:54.100 --> 00:18:55.090
MARK MANDEL: --doing yourself.

00:18:55.090 --> 00:18:56.131
MILES WARD: That's right.

00:18:56.131 --> 00:18:59.879
I think we have an environment
that really radically de-risks

00:18:59.879 --> 00:19:01.420
that kind of
experimentation, I think

00:19:01.420 --> 00:19:04.210
it's a fun place to build new
things and to try stuff out.

00:19:04.210 --> 00:19:06.570
But I think some people have
a certain amount of budget

00:19:06.570 --> 00:19:08.153
and a certain amount
of time and focus

00:19:08.153 --> 00:19:09.700
for that kind of
experimentation,

00:19:09.700 --> 00:19:12.294
and then the rest of the time
is spent on the obnoxious stuff

00:19:12.294 --> 00:19:13.210
that should just work.

00:19:13.210 --> 00:19:15.430
We're trying to go through
a step-by-step walk

00:19:15.430 --> 00:19:18.760
through each of those pieces and
make it so that they just work.

00:19:18.760 --> 00:19:21.010
Like, backing up
a database is not

00:19:21.010 --> 00:19:24.220
something that is a creative
and advanced expression of--

00:19:24.220 --> 00:19:27.836
MARK MANDEL: Oh, I do that
on the weekend, so much fun.

00:19:27.836 --> 00:19:29.710
MILES WARD: It should
probably have a manual.

00:19:29.710 --> 00:19:30.270
MARK MANDEL: Yep.

00:19:30.270 --> 00:19:31.186
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:19:31.186 --> 00:19:33.880
Now, you obviously speak
to a lot of customers

00:19:33.880 --> 00:19:35.530
and you've been
here at Cloud Next.

00:19:35.530 --> 00:19:37.669
How have the experiences
for people you've

00:19:37.669 --> 00:19:38.710
talked to have been like?

00:19:38.710 --> 00:19:40.085
What has been the
response you've

00:19:40.085 --> 00:19:42.242
had to the venue and event?

00:19:42.242 --> 00:19:44.200
MILES WARD: I think
there's a couple of phrases

00:19:44.200 --> 00:19:45.656
that have come out
of our keynotes

00:19:45.656 --> 00:19:47.530
and come out of some of
our big presentations

00:19:47.530 --> 00:19:50.830
that have had a really big
impact on the perspective

00:19:50.830 --> 00:19:52.750
from the customers
that I talked with.

00:19:52.750 --> 00:19:54.520
I went to three different
customer dinners

00:19:54.520 --> 00:19:55.580
just last night.

00:19:55.580 --> 00:19:57.580
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Did you
eat dinner three times?

00:19:57.580 --> 00:20:00.701
MILES WARD: Well, you know,
you can have a lot of salad

00:20:00.701 --> 00:20:02.950
and everybody sort of thinks
that you're participating

00:20:02.950 --> 00:20:04.830
in the fullness of the chow.

00:20:04.830 --> 00:20:09.230
So what I heard from customers
was three basic things.

00:20:09.230 --> 00:20:11.570
First was from
the first keynote,

00:20:11.570 --> 00:20:14.252
seeing HSBC on stage,
that's an account that I

00:20:14.252 --> 00:20:15.710
did a lot of work
with, I was super

00:20:15.710 --> 00:20:17.630
excited to see them
take the stage.

00:20:17.630 --> 00:20:22.010
Seeing how clearly for
them the value of cloud

00:20:22.010 --> 00:20:24.890
was associated with the
speed with which they could

00:20:24.890 --> 00:20:26.470
do things, like
what they're seeing

00:20:26.470 --> 00:20:29.000
in every one of the
examples and demonstrations.

00:20:29.000 --> 00:20:30.620
And this stuff is fast.

00:20:30.620 --> 00:20:33.970
It's not fast like 100 million
IOPS or something like that.

00:20:33.970 --> 00:20:37.100
It's fast like it takes
Evernote three months

00:20:37.100 --> 00:20:39.410
to move, it takes
Lush a month to move,

00:20:39.410 --> 00:20:43.070
it takes Ocado six hours
to rewrite their ETL.

00:20:43.070 --> 00:20:46.827
These statistics are now the
material to go, wait a minute,

00:20:46.827 --> 00:20:48.410
I'm planning and
budgeting in the back

00:20:48.410 --> 00:20:50.390
of my mind on like
Netflix scales,

00:20:50.390 --> 00:20:52.910
where it's going to take
seven years to migrate.

00:20:52.910 --> 00:20:55.450
If I can do this stuff in
months instead of years,

00:20:55.450 --> 00:20:56.950
the economics are
totally different,

00:20:56.950 --> 00:20:59.464
the value proposition
is totally different.

00:20:59.464 --> 00:21:01.130
If I go around
enterprise to enterprise,

00:21:01.130 --> 00:21:02.671
even little startups
that are already

00:21:02.671 --> 00:21:04.100
implemented in
other places, they

00:21:04.100 --> 00:21:07.670
say, wow, I didn't realize
that it was that much faster.

00:21:07.670 --> 00:21:10.850
That's a big deal to me, that
really changes my perspective.

00:21:10.850 --> 00:21:11.930
So that's awesome.

00:21:11.930 --> 00:21:13.410
That's super awesome to see.

00:21:13.410 --> 00:21:14.826
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
There's something

00:21:14.826 --> 00:21:17.420
to be said there about
also the exponential growth

00:21:17.420 --> 00:21:18.980
that you can have.

00:21:18.980 --> 00:21:21.730
like PokemonGo, Niantic.

00:21:21.730 --> 00:21:22.945
MILES WARD: Yeah.

00:21:22.945 --> 00:21:24.620
To talk about
exponential growth,

00:21:24.620 --> 00:21:28.240
I was at the GCP event-- they
didn't call it Next yet--

00:21:28.240 --> 00:21:29.120
three years ago.

00:21:29.120 --> 00:21:29.670
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Oh, yeah.

00:21:29.670 --> 00:21:30.640
I remember those.

00:21:30.640 --> 00:21:32.650
MILES WARD: 375 people there.

00:21:32.650 --> 00:21:34.247
I went to the one
last year, presented

00:21:34.247 --> 00:21:36.080
a bunch, built a bunch
of stuff there, built

00:21:36.080 --> 00:21:38.010
the tree house over there
for that [INAUDIBLE].

00:21:38.010 --> 00:21:38.770
MARK MANDEL: Oh, yeah.

00:21:38.770 --> 00:21:40.436
MILES WARD: There
were 2,600 people that

00:21:40.436 --> 00:21:42.140
came through the door for that.

00:21:42.140 --> 00:21:45.800
There are like almost
14,000 people here.

00:21:45.800 --> 00:21:47.890
MARK MANDEL: It's crazy.

00:21:47.890 --> 00:21:51.140
MILES WARD: It's 10x and
then 5x to say, OK, fine, 3x.

00:21:51.140 --> 00:21:53.960
We're going to have to put
in intermediary floors inside

00:21:53.960 --> 00:21:57.350
of Moscone to fit enough
humans to watch all the stuff

00:21:57.350 --> 00:21:58.520
that we're building up.

00:21:58.520 --> 00:22:00.022
That's outrageous growth.

00:22:00.022 --> 00:22:01.730
FRANCESC CAMPOY: It's
amazing also seeing

00:22:01.730 --> 00:22:05.330
all the number of other partners
that we have all around.

00:22:05.330 --> 00:22:06.805
It is amazing.

00:22:06.805 --> 00:22:09.180
MARK MANDEL: Even just walking
through the startup space,

00:22:09.180 --> 00:22:10.721
there's like 10, 15
of them in there,

00:22:10.721 --> 00:22:14.540
just all of them doing each
individual cool things.

00:22:14.540 --> 00:22:16.880
FRANCESC CAMPOY: What
other things have you

00:22:16.880 --> 00:22:19.540
heard from customers that
they're really excited about?

00:22:19.540 --> 00:22:23.600
Was there any specific
announcement or something

00:22:23.600 --> 00:22:26.690
like that that they were like,
oh, that thing was amazing?

00:22:26.690 --> 00:22:29.420
MILES WARD: I was surprised--

00:22:29.420 --> 00:22:31.340
I know the degree
to which we hold it

00:22:31.340 --> 00:22:36.060
as sort of its own
unique class of things.

00:22:36.060 --> 00:22:40.094
But the messaging around
Spanner has landed.

00:22:40.094 --> 00:22:41.510
And when I talk
to customers, they

00:22:41.510 --> 00:22:44.310
go, look, man, I read
that paper five years ago.

00:22:44.310 --> 00:22:46.790
I know that you guys had the
crown jewels hiding someplace.

00:22:46.790 --> 00:22:48.650
I know that that's the one.

00:22:48.650 --> 00:22:50.330
We are diving in,
figuring it out.

00:22:50.330 --> 00:22:53.660
And I'm watching on the inside
as we work with customers,

00:22:53.660 --> 00:22:55.280
and look at the metrics.

00:22:55.280 --> 00:23:01.460
The growth on Spanner adoption
in weeks is stupefying.

00:23:01.460 --> 00:23:04.940
I've got engineers on the
SRE team changing trajectory

00:23:04.940 --> 00:23:07.352
of focus to deploy more
and more infrastructure

00:23:07.352 --> 00:23:08.810
into the Spanner
hosting just to be

00:23:08.810 --> 00:23:10.230
able to keep pace with demand.

00:23:10.230 --> 00:23:13.700
And as our customers
are talking about it,

00:23:13.700 --> 00:23:16.850
I think they got the message up
front about the idea of, yeah,

00:23:16.850 --> 00:23:18.650
I can do globally
consistent transactions.

00:23:18.650 --> 00:23:20.270
I can make it so that
when I write something

00:23:20.270 --> 00:23:21.920
on one side of the planet, on
the other side of the planet

00:23:21.920 --> 00:23:23.807
I'm not messed up
and out of sync.

00:23:23.807 --> 00:23:25.390
But I don't think
they really realized

00:23:25.390 --> 00:23:30.480
that that came along with super,
super fast, super, super big.

00:23:30.480 --> 00:23:35.050
Like, the simpler message of,
yep, millions of IOPS is OK,

00:23:35.050 --> 00:23:37.480
yup, doing it at
gazillions of bytes

00:23:37.480 --> 00:23:39.980
you can put terabytes in there,
is fine, petabytes in there,

00:23:39.980 --> 00:23:40.860
is whatever.

00:23:40.860 --> 00:23:42.940
So like, I get both?

00:23:42.940 --> 00:23:45.220
Wait a minute, how--
why-- how am I-- what?

00:23:45.220 --> 00:23:46.790
It seems very
surprising to them.

00:23:46.790 --> 00:23:49.820
So it's cool that we've
then able to really hammer

00:23:49.820 --> 00:23:54.110
on both of those messages and
I'm seeing businesses saying,

00:23:54.110 --> 00:23:56.720
it's weird, you're right,
I don't like my Oracle

00:23:56.720 --> 00:23:59.239
and I'm not super stoked about
my mySQL, and all of those

00:23:59.239 --> 00:24:00.530
are sort of a pain in the neck.

00:24:00.530 --> 00:24:01.970
Like, maybe I
should try this out.

00:24:01.970 --> 00:24:04.345
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So what's
your point of view on the fact

00:24:04.345 --> 00:24:06.030
that Spanner is still beta?

00:24:06.030 --> 00:24:08.000
Does that mean that
people are not using it?

00:24:08.000 --> 00:24:10.280
It seems like it's
the other way around.

00:24:10.280 --> 00:24:11.900
MILES WARD: No.

00:24:11.900 --> 00:24:14.250
In my experience, what I've
seen-- you have to imagine,

00:24:14.250 --> 00:24:17.140
there's a range of products that
are being built by Google Cloud

00:24:17.140 --> 00:24:20.720
Platform, that are being built
for cloud right now as we

00:24:20.720 --> 00:24:22.400
speak, built from scratch.

00:24:22.400 --> 00:24:25.430
The GLP API is not that way.

00:24:25.430 --> 00:24:29.250
We take a service from G
Suite and we externalize it

00:24:29.250 --> 00:24:31.880
as a dedicated service
that customers can use.

00:24:31.880 --> 00:24:33.890
Spanner is an
example that's more

00:24:33.890 --> 00:24:36.590
that way, where we have
a running production

00:24:36.590 --> 00:24:39.050
system handling millions
and millions of transactions

00:24:39.050 --> 00:24:41.690
a second, serving hundreds
of Google businesses.

00:24:41.690 --> 00:24:44.860
It is highly mature in
its operations model,

00:24:44.860 --> 00:24:47.930
our SLOs are well defined, we
have a clear standard service

00:24:47.930 --> 00:24:48.830
there.

00:24:48.830 --> 00:24:50.940
The new part is that
we're charging for it.

00:24:50.940 --> 00:24:52.040
There's this little
billing component

00:24:52.040 --> 00:24:53.060
that's got to get
slapped on, you

00:24:53.060 --> 00:24:54.530
got to have an API
that's externalized

00:24:54.530 --> 00:24:55.610
that you have to slap on.

00:24:55.610 --> 00:24:57.950
And we need to test those
to the same level of rigor

00:24:57.950 --> 00:24:59.510
as the core operations.

00:24:59.510 --> 00:25:01.910
I think in the
database world, for me

00:25:01.910 --> 00:25:05.480
the big risk or the big terror
is losing data or having

00:25:05.480 --> 00:25:06.394
data corrupted.

00:25:06.394 --> 00:25:08.810
And I think those are the
places where the new layers that

00:25:08.810 --> 00:25:10.100
are part of Spanner to
make it so that they

00:25:10.100 --> 00:25:11.930
can be a public
service, they just

00:25:11.930 --> 00:25:13.700
don't affect that part at all.

00:25:13.700 --> 00:25:18.040
So I see customers able to
do that calculus and say,

00:25:18.040 --> 00:25:19.850
yeah, let's dive in,
let's figure that out.

00:25:19.850 --> 00:25:20.870
MARK MANDEL: Since you're
on the customer side,

00:25:20.870 --> 00:25:21.680
now I'm just curious.

00:25:21.680 --> 00:25:24.013
Are you seeing customers--
are they jumping on the beta?

00:25:24.013 --> 00:25:26.900
Being able to kind of provide
feedback and push the direction

00:25:26.900 --> 00:25:28.232
of where the product goes?

00:25:28.232 --> 00:25:28.940
MILES WARD: Yeah.

00:25:28.940 --> 00:25:30.800
I had a great
conversation on Twitter

00:25:30.800 --> 00:25:34.610
yesterday that one of the parts
of the design of the product,

00:25:34.610 --> 00:25:37.820
in order to be able to meet the
tight SLOs that it's designed

00:25:37.820 --> 00:25:39.380
for, to be able to
deliver the kind

00:25:39.380 --> 00:25:42.050
of transactional consistency,
it's not multitenant.

00:25:42.050 --> 00:25:44.810
So we ask you to build
individual nodes,

00:25:44.810 --> 00:25:46.760
which we call on
our side, and like

00:25:46.760 --> 00:25:49.780
any good relational
structure that's replicated,

00:25:49.780 --> 00:25:52.460
your real default there
is to deploy three nodes.

00:25:52.460 --> 00:25:55.940
So the minimum cost to run
it 24/7 with those three base

00:25:55.940 --> 00:25:58.200
nodes putting lots of
gigabytes of data in there,

00:25:58.200 --> 00:26:00.410
it's about 2,000 bucks a month.

00:26:00.410 --> 00:26:02.270
So when I talk to
developers, they

00:26:02.270 --> 00:26:05.667
say, look, when I'm in prod,
I'm going to give you $200,000

00:26:05.667 --> 00:26:07.250
a month because I'm
going to want this

00:26:07.250 --> 00:26:08.900
at the size of the moon.

00:26:08.900 --> 00:26:11.384
But right now I want to
mess around on my laptop

00:26:11.384 --> 00:26:12.800
and I want to mess
around in a way

00:26:12.800 --> 00:26:14.841
that I know is compatible
with the primary thing.

00:26:14.841 --> 00:26:17.420
Or, fine, if it's impossible
to put on my laptop,

00:26:17.420 --> 00:26:20.156
give me something that I can
run on a micro, or whatever,

00:26:20.156 --> 00:26:22.030
so that it's at a price
when it's compatible.

00:26:22.030 --> 00:26:24.580
And I know our
product managers, they

00:26:24.580 --> 00:26:26.330
made an analysis of
the market and they're

00:26:26.330 --> 00:26:28.760
looking at what technology
they have available.

00:26:28.760 --> 00:26:31.190
I think we were
really eager to take

00:26:31.190 --> 00:26:33.950
what we know of as the exact
best practice for a product

00:26:33.950 --> 00:26:37.370
that already has a reputation
years before it's launched

00:26:37.370 --> 00:26:40.520
and make sure that that
product behaves exactly the way

00:26:40.520 --> 00:26:43.160
customers have dreamed
about for years.

00:26:43.160 --> 00:26:46.187
Now they've got their
hands on the real deal.

00:26:46.187 --> 00:26:48.020
The feedback that I've
heard is clear enough

00:26:48.020 --> 00:26:50.240
that it's obvious to me
that we'll go through and do

00:26:50.240 --> 00:26:52.880
the work to create something
that's designed for developer

00:26:52.880 --> 00:26:55.010
consumption so that the
experimentation can happen

00:26:55.010 --> 00:26:57.675
without costing a couple grand.

00:26:57.675 --> 00:26:59.300
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Is
